From 946a86e3e312d3a62850f48bd1e034c9a336d696 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 06:41:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] p2-dominate-and-harden

P2 (days 22-60): performance, security, and the two surfaces that compound.

P2.5 LCP. The hero image was fetched from Unsplash AFTER hydration: ship HTML
with no image, download the bundle, hydrate, call /api/backgrounds, which called
the Unsplash search API, which returned a URL, which was then requested. Six
sequential steps, two across the public internet, before LCP could begin - and
the preload scanner never saw any of it, so fetchpriority was decorative. With
UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY unset it rendered no hero at all. HeroBackdrop replaces it
with a StaticImageData import: fingerprinted into _next/static, real
<link rel=preload>, blur placeholder generated at build, zero JS, zero third
parties. It is one of the 36 images already bundled and published in the
collection section of the same page, so nothing new is claimed - and it is our
own asset rather than a stock photo of somebody else's floor at the top of a
page arguing that everything here is first-party. FloorCatalog, MachineCatalog
and FigureRotator are now code-split with ssr:true - the markup still renders on
the server so the indexable corpus is untouched; only the JavaScript defers.

P2.1 Video. ProcessVideo is a facade, not an iframe: poster is local, the
youtube-nocookie iframe is created on click, so a cold page pays nothing. Gated
on NEXT_PUBLIC_YOUTUBE_PROCESS_ID; unset - today - it shows the poster, a "Film
coming" badge and two sentences that teach what the film would teach. No
third-party footage stands in for ours.

P2.2 /design/spec. The configurator's output as a document: species, Janka,
finish, pattern, area, indicative range, NAP. Print-styled so the browser's own
Save-as-PDF produces the file - a real PDF on every platform, a shareable URL,
and no binary dependency to maintain. Config travels in the querystring, falls
back to ew-design-v1. noindex: it is one visitor's working file.

P2.3 /refer. The cheapest customer this business will ever acquire. Terms live
once in content/referral.ts and verify-referral.mjs fails the build if the
figure is typed elsewhere OR if the reward is ever published without its
condition - this is the one number on the site that is a debt rather than an
estimate. The referrer's consent is required by the schema, logged on capture,
and the route emails the estimating desk rather than auto-contacting the person
referred: CASL's referral exemption is one message, and only where a real
relationship exists.

P2.4 CSP promoted to ENFORCED without unsafe-eval. Nothing here evals; it was an
inherited default and it is the primitive most injected payloads want.
unsafe-inline stays in script-src deliberately - removing it needs a nonce, a
nonce needs per-request rendering, and that would convert 287 prerendered pages
to dynamic, costing the caching in P0.1 and the LCP work above to tighten one
directive Next's own bootstrap would need an exception from anyway. A stricter
report-only policy ships beside it so the console measures what a future nonce
migration must cover. /api/csp-report gives the reports somewhere to land -
previously report-only had no report-uri, so browsers evaluated the policy and
threw the answer away.

Also removed the Meta Pixel entirely. NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID was never set,
so it never fired, while /privacy declared Meta a processor of visitors' data
and the CSP carried two Meta origins for it. Over-declaring where personal data
goes is a false statement, not a safe default. Loader, consent toggle, CSP
entries and legal declaration removed together; consent version bumped to 2
because a stored decision about a processor that no longer exists is not
consent. 7 processors declared now, all real.

P2.6 AI pack. llms.txt gains a "Facts you can cite" block in the first screen -
NAP, hours, founding year, all three price bands, and the review citation with
its read date - every value derived, no literals. An answer engine reads the top
of a document and stops; those facts were scattered across 300 lines.
verify-knowledge-parity.mjs is the guard the brief asked for: llms.txt, ai.txt,
llms-full.txt and /api/knowledge must DERIVE every price, NAP and review figure,
and none may emit aggregateRating. A site that states one price to people and
another to machines is worse than one that publishes nothing.

Verified: all 43 guards pass (40 + verify-referral + verify-knowledge-parity,
both in the verify chain); tsc error count identical to main (107, all
sandbox-blocked Prisma engine, zero in P2 files).

